  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 226 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nSaturday, the high pressure that has been hanging on for dear life \nas it is pinched between the two troughs will begin to influence the \nOzarks. Highs warm a few degrees into the upper 40s and low 50s. \nWinds become light and turn west/southwesterly as the high pressure \ncenter passes by to the north across central Missouri. High cloud \ncover begins to intrude from the southwest ahead of the troughing \nthat starts pushing into Texas.\n\nBy Sunday, ensembles seem to agree that the incoming shortwave \nundercuts the ridge as it moves across the southern Plains Sunday \nand Monday. However, there is disagreement regarding magnitude and \ntrack. The NBM continues to trend down on PoPs for our area, and \ncurrently limits them to 15-20% for our extreme southwestern-most \ncounties. It also clears them out before temperatures could drop low \nenough to result in any snow flurries. The Canadian ensemble is most \ngenerous in giving areas southwest of Springfield up to two tenths \nof an inch of rain while the European ensemble brings only two \nhundredths. The official deterministic forecast (in this case, \nthe NBM) currently depicts zero QPF.\n\nRegardless of the precipitation outcome of the cutoff low, ridging \nlooks to take hold once more to begin the work week. Temperatures \nsteadily trend up to near 60 again by midweek, several degrees above \nour normals in the mid-50s. There is surprisingly good agreement on \na frontal passage midweek that *could* result in precipitation, but \nprobabilities are low for now and it would be irresponsible to \nassert anything for sure at this lead time.",
